sorry --- I assumed most people had compiled X applications.... (I do a lot 
more than most people and I just forgot it isn't common knowlege).

Imakefiles are "independant makefiles", and are translated to Makefiles by the
"xmkmf" ("X make makefiles") utility. This was done to incorporate vendor
X configuration files into the resulting Makefile. The normal cycle to to 
build X utilities is:

1.	xmkmf			- to build the top level makefile
2. 	make Makefiles	- to build subdirectory makefiles (if any). In this
					particular case, there are no extensive builds, so this step is a no-op
3.	make			- which builds the application

The xmkmf is usually a shell script that uses the C preprocessor to perform
macro substitutions (the vendor specified locations for binaries, libraries,
include files, default application configurations) and conditional output
generation.

If it is not in your search path, it is normally found in the X11 binary 
directory (/usr/bin/X11/xmkmf on RH).
